{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"635106c0ff614153","type":"group","x":-880,"y":-1160,"width":2055,"height":1220},
		{"id":"e4a6a90bdc0cbc86","type":"text","text":"## Memory Stages:\n- Sensory Memory\n- Short-Term Memory\n- Long-Term Memory\n\nThese are the different stages in which information is kept within the brain.","x":-560,"y":-1140,"width":263,"height":380},
		{"id":"3748df90889e6c81","type":"text","text":"## Cognitive Stages:\n- Input\n- Attention\n- Encoding\n- Retrieval\n\nThey control how information flows through the different memory stages.","x":-860,"y":-1140,"width":263,"height":380},
		{"id":"51e571b8fc69384b","type":"text","text":"## Optimal Learning System\n\nAn optimal learning system is one that allows information to flow through this process without being forgotten. It means stimulating each \"gateway\" and step to strengthen and allow data to be retained and have deep understanding.","x":-105,"y":-1140,"width":468,"height":223},
		{"id":"5107c1e6c37fafc2","type":"text","text":"## Stimuli\n\n- Sights\n- Colors\n- Sounds\n- Vision\n- Environment","x":-860,"y":-612,"width":220,"height":236},
		{"id":"ad74c3a74cd733fe","type":"text","text":"- Accessing stored memory when needed.\n- This tests and strengthens the ability to recall information.","x":459,"y":-1140,"width":357,"height":174},
		{"id":"ddd67fa012e8ab11","type":"text","text":"## Retrieval","x":538,"y":-920,"width":200,"height":50},
		{"id":"30b9598f9bcf40e8","type":"text","text":"## Attention","x":-15,"y":-524,"width":175,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"cbc020ba22b7b5ac","type":"text","text":"- Allows information that you are currently focusing on through.\n- (e.g. the word you're reading, picture you're looking at.)","x":-106,"y":-820,"width":358,"height":174},
		{"id":"1af2a067ea6b3b8e","type":"text","text":"## Sensory Memory\n### The Current Snapshot\n- Everything coming from our senses\n- Lasts 0.1-0.2s\n- Extensive space","x":-355,"y":-612,"width":250,"height":236},
		{"id":"e1f18ad518435e93","type":"text","text":"- Data that is isolated or does not have many connection (unimportant)\n- \"A characteristic feature of higher-order learning is that information is integrated, not isloated.\" - Justin Sung","x":404,"y":-180,"width":358,"height":220},
		{"id":"99c083c55e2a18ab","type":"text","text":"- Forgotten when stored data is not maintained.\n- Maintaining memory can be done through retrieval.\n- Or recalling information to strengthen understanding and memory.","x":797,"y":-180,"width":358,"height":220},
		{"id":"0575acaa828f0a94","type":"text","text":"# Forgotten","x":512,"y":-310,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"8e38d26d117f7567","type":"text","text":"# Forgotten","x":797,"y":-310,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"d8c81ce00c935d39","type":"text","text":"## Encoding","x":557,"y":-524,"width":160,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"e587706544736752","type":"text","text":"## Short-Term Memory\n\n### The Thinking Zone\n\n- Location of all information we are currently focusing on.","x":225,"y":-612,"width":245,"height":236},
		{"id":"bea0901a8beac93b","type":"text","text":"- Encoding turns raw data into \"readable\" data.\n- It does this by creating connections and group between existing knowledge and the raw data. ","x":459,"y":-820,"width":357,"height":174},
		{"id":"29d179d7d3b80b31","type":"file","file":"10 Growing Notes/Project OCLS/1.2 - Cognitive Load Theory.md","x":147,"y":160,"width":400,"height":400},
		{"id":"9a88a50463375d08","type":"text","text":"## Sources\n\n[What is Information Processing Theory? Stages, Models & Limitations for 2026](http://research.com/education/what-is-information-processing-theory)\n\n[7 Years of Building a Learning System in 12 minutes](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6GTt10GDWII)\n\n[Watch This to Force Your Brain to Study FASTER](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hz4JKqoG_Mc)\n\n[What is Information Processing Theory](https://onlineprograms.ecu.edu/blog/information-processing-theory/)","x":-860,"y":-1860,"width":440,"height":640},
		{"id":"74a0c2ad49dcfbcf","type":"text","text":"## Input","x":-597,"y":-524,"width":125,"height":60},
		{"id":"819085f5ec23db42","type":"text","text":"- Data that is not being focused on.\n- (e.g. the color of the wall, random, object aside desk.)","x":-106,"y":-180,"width":358,"height":174},
		{"id":"9b6917f8f1499659","type":"text","text":"# Forgotten","x":-52,"y":-310,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"2e91b45c43eeb7bc","type":"text","text":"## Long-Term Memory","x":797,"y":-612,"width":250,"height":236},
		{"id":"0aedfbba511a13a5","type":"text","text":"## Storage","x":842,"y":-524,"width":160,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"2183b41cdbd17df3","type":"text","text":"## Storage","x":842,"y":-523,"width":160,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"e29ebac3093c76de","type":"file","file":"10 Growing Notes/Project OCLS/1.3 - Schema Theory.md","x":1520,"y":-694,"width":400,"height":400},
		{"id":"4d56b98c44a421d1","type":"text","text":"# Information Processing Theory\n### The Backbone of Cognitive Learning Theory\n\nThis theory explains how the human mind intakes, interprets, processes, and retrieves information. Aka, how do people learn, or retain knowledge? What is process that happens when we learn new things?","x":-34,"y":-1719,"width":363,"height":359},
		{"id":"c0855e53da55eeb2","x":616,"y":-1739,"width":400,"height":400,"type":"file","file":"10 Growing Notes/Project OCLS/1.0 - Cognitive Learning Theory.canvas"}
